  "id": "DrugBank_DB01077 - MONDO_9394",
  "verbalization": "Etidronic Acid is implicated in the pathogenesis of Juvenile Paget Disease through a multi-step mechanism involving bone-related indications. The evidence suggests that Etidronic Acid may act as a precursor in the treatment of Bone Paget Disease, with its use linked to the treatment of Risedronic Acid and Zoledronic Acid, both of which are known to inhibit bone resorption and are used in the management of Paget Disease. The final scores from the visible paths indicate a strong, though not absolute, association between Etidronic Acid and Juvenile Paget Disease, likely through its role in the treatment of Bone Paget Disease. The synergistic interaction with Trebananib and subsequent effect on Alendronic Acid further supports a potential mechanistic link, though the exact role of these interactions remains to be elucidated. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Etidronic Acid to be repurposed in the treatment of Juvenile Paget Disease, provided further experimental validation confirms the direct therapeutic effect.",
